# CODEOWNERS — the only part of a lane split that works even when the rule is
# forgotten. Last match wins, so order matters: put the broad rules first and the
# re-assertions last. Fill in once lanes and owners are decided; delete if the
# project has a single owner.
#
# ⚠ A line here can be syntactically perfect and route nowhere. GitHub does not request
# review from a code owner who lacks WRITE access to the repository, and does not warn:
# the syntax check passes, the review request simply goes to somebody else. The only
# signal is who actually gets asked — so after adding an owner, open a pull request that
# touches their paths and check that they were requested.
#
# Example shape — the org lane vocabulary is infra · app · product and a project takes the
# subset it needs (`issue-flow`, and references/adopting-a-lane.md for the order to do it
# in). Replace the paths with the ones each lane actually owns; a lane given a whole tree
# because it mostly belongs there generates review requests that carry no decision for it.
# *             @org/team-or-user
# /infra/       @infra-owner
# /docs/specs/  @product-owner
